<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!DOCTYPE page SYSTEM "entities.dtd" >
<xpage:page xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xpage="http://eprints.org/ep3/xpage" xmlns:epc="http://eprints.org/ep3/control">

<xpage:title>What would be best practice for authors choosing their own keywords?</xpage:title>
    <xpage:body>
		<div>
			<p>
			When submitting papers to journals and conferences, authors a frequently asked to add their own keywords. At that point in the process, no advice is provided about good practice in choosing keywords. These keywords may not intended for indexing, as they are uncontrolled. They will help indexers, and may be used directly. There are several things that authors should consider, as a matter of good practice for long-term discoverability in any archive or database that lists their work:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
				Do not use the journal's own subject area as a keyword. In a construction management journal, "construction management" adds no discriminatory value. It would have to appear on every paper or none.
				</li>
				<li>
				Do not use "construction industry" unless the paper is genuinely a sector-level economic or industry analysis. A paper that happens to be set within a national construction industry context is not an industry analysis.
				</li>
				<li>
				Do include geographic identifiers (e.g. Nigeria, South Africa) when findings are geographically specific. These are frequently omitted and are genuinely useful for filtering.
				</li>
				<li>
				<b>Do not use acronyms in keywords or titles!</b> <a href="/info-acronyms.html">Acronyms collide</a> across disciplines and even within construction management research: BIM alone is used for British Institute of Management, Building Information Model, Building Information Modelling, and others. A reader searching for one will retrieve papers about another, and a reader searching for another may miss yours entirely. Define acronyms in the body text; do not rely on them to carry meaning in keywords or titles.
				</li>
				<li>Do choose keywords that describe the research as well as its subject. A balanced set of keywords will identify what was studied, the concepts the paper draws on, the theoretical framing and the techniques used to collect and analyse the data. Keywords about the subject alone do not tell a reader what kind of study the paper reports.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>To see how the wording of a draft title and abstract relates to the vocabulary of this catalogue, try the <a href="/cgi/checkabstract">Check your own abstract</a> page. It lists the index terms your wording matches, grouped by the five facets of the taxonomy, and helps you pick from them the terms that properly represent your work. Nothing you enter is stored.</p>
		</div>
		<div style="margin-bottom: 1em">
		  <epc:phrase ref="contact-footer"/>
		</div>

